onstructing representing random variables. Its chief point is entailed in Eq. (2). However, this procedure just yields the variables and does not answer interesting questions such as: are there independent representations or representations from a given family of distributions? These questions were relevant in the older literature, not in the sense of existence but in the sense of best approximations. For example, Thurstonean scaling is the search of normally distributed random variables which sort of best represent choice data. From the modern point of view these techniques are not sufficient because they do not deal with, let alone answer, the question of the existence of a representation.\n\nIn the present paper I will take up the question of the existence of independent representations. The standard way of constructing random variables which fit the choice data yields dependent variables. Can it be modified in such a way that the resulting variables are jointly independent?\n\nRandom utility theory is covered in the literature in a variety of different areas such as economics (McFadden, 1987), decision theory (Luce, 2000), psychology (Colonius, 1984), and others. The perspective taken in the present paper seems to be initiated by Marschak (1960) and Block and Marschak (1960). Comprehensive introductions into random variable representations are Marley (1990) and Suppes et al. (1989, Chap. 17). Once the importance of the geometric aspect was recognized, the focus of attention switched to the description of the polytopes in question, cf. Fishburn (1992) for an overview. Generalizations of various aspects are natural and are discussed in the literature. The most general framework seems to be expounded by Niederée and Heyer (1997). The convex hull of these points is P_{LO}^{n}.\n\nClearly, given such a 0/1-vector π we can reconstruct the permutation σ∈S_{n} such that π_{σ}=π. For this reason we write somewhat loosely π∈S_{n}.\n\nThe properties of P_{LO}^{n} are investigated in many papers, primarily in connection with optimization tasks, cf. Grötschel et al. (1985) and Reinelt (1985). For this investigation its facets are most important. A face of a polytope is the intersection of it with a hyperplane which has the polytope completely in one of the closed halfspaces defined by the hyperplane. A facet is a face of maximal dimension. For polytopes the reader is referred to Ziegler (1995). An overview of the connection of P_{LO}^{n} to random utility theory is given by Fishburn (1992), Suck (1992) and Koppen (1995).\n\nIn the present paper we do not contribute to P_{LO}^{n}. We are concerned with the subset which can be represented by independent random variables. This subset is denoted by I_{LO}^{n}.\n\nThe convex hull of a subset is denoted by conv(X). A set is called star-shaped with respect to x∈X if for any y∈X the line connecting x and y belongs to X. A convex set is star-shaped with respect to all points it contains.\n\nA random variable is of finite support if the set of values it attains with positive probability is finite. When this set consists of two points it is called a Bernoulli variate. A discrete random variable has finite or denumerable support where in the latter case the values with positive probability are isolated points in \\text{R}.\n\nTwo random variables U, V are non-coincident if P(U=V)=0. Thus, continuous variables are always non-coincident, whereas for discrete variables certain precautions must be observed if one wants this property fulfilled. In random utility theory we look for variables satisfying Eq. (1); then non-coincidence is mandated by p_{ij}=1−p_{ji}.\n\nClearly, I_{LO}^{n}⊆P_{LO}^{n}. At first\n\n### Necessary and sufficient conditions for independent representations\n\nIn the preceding section we argued that Eq. (6) is very useful for deriving conditions for independent representability of binary choice matrices. Now we present two results which together give a theorem first proven by Trybula (1961).\n\nTheorem 2\n\nLet p=(p_{ij}) be a binary choice matrix on n elements which allows an independent representation. Then for all distinct i, j, k∈{1, 2,…, n}\n\nProof\n\nBy approximating ∫F_{i} dF_{j} by simple functions, i.e. functions with only a finite\n\n### The mixture technique\n\nIf one has constructed independent representations for a few points in I_{LO}^{n} one can find new points in I_{LO}^{n} by mixing these representations. The random variables with the corresponding mixture distributions yield the independent representations of these new points. This technique will be employed to derive a few useful results on I_{LO}^{n}. The procedure will be developed in the present section.\n\nConsider a collection of k≥1 random vectors with n independent components.\n\n### Parametric results\n\nThurstonean scaling, for example, assumes representability by normally distributed random variables. What are the conditions for the existence of such a representation?\n\nTo give an example, are there normally distributed random variables in the following case:The answer is negative, because for some i, j, k all of the probabilities exceed , namely p_{13}=2/3, p_{34}=2/3, and p_{41}=5/9. Thus, the choice probabilities violate weak stochastic transitivity.\n\nIt is an easy\n\n### Discussion and open problems\n\nIn Section 3 we started the project of direct constructions of representing random variables, i.e. without the detour on P_{LO}^{n}. However, the linear ordering polytope always looms behind the development. For example, the condition of Theorem 2 is a modification of the facets of P_{LO}^{n} which stem from the triangle condition. Moreover, the mixture technique works best when one starts with the vertices of a facet of P_{LO}^{n} (which are in I_{LO}^{n} according to Theorem 1). Therefore, it seems plausible to\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nControl systems on Lie groups (https://scispace.com/papers/co