Sussmann. “Control systems on Lie groups”. In: *Journal of Differential Equations* 12.2 (Sept. 1972), pp. 313–329. DOI: 10.1016/0022-0396(72)90035-6.\n\n## A Derivation of the model\n\nThe Hubbard dimer model describes a quantum system with two sites occupied by spin-1/2 fermions (recall that electrons are spin-1/2 fermions).\n\nWe denote by \\(\\mathcal{H}_1\\) the one-particle state space of the Hubbard dimer, that is the four-dimensional complex vector space\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{H}_1 = \\mathbb{C}\\,|\\uparrow 0\\rangle \\oplus \\mathbb{C}\\,|\\downarrow 0\\rangle \\oplus \\mathbb{C}\\,|0\\uparrow\\rangle \\oplus \\mathbb{C}\\,|0\\downarrow\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nwhere the four orthonormal vectors \\(|\\uparrow 0\\rangle\\), \\(|\\downarrow 0\\rangle\\), \\(|0\\uparrow\\rangle\\) and \\(|0\\downarrow\\rangle\\) respectively represent one-particle states in which the particle is on the first site with spin up, on the first site with spin down, on the second site with spin up, and on the second site with spin down. Since two fermions cannot occupy the same quantum state by virtue of the Pauli principle, the Hubbard dimer can host between 0 and 4 particles. The zero-particle state space is\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{H}_0 = \\mathbb{C}|\\mathrm{vac}\\rangle,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(|\\mathrm{vac}\\rangle\\) is the vacuum (a normalized quantum state with no particle), the \\(N\\)-particle state spaces, \\(2 \\leq N \\leq 4\\), are given by\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{H}_N = \\bigwedge^N \\mathcal{H}_1\n\\]\n\n(antisymmetrized tensor product of \\(N\\) copies of the one-particle state space), and the Fock space is defined as\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{F} := \\mathcal{H}_0 \\oplus \\mathcal{H}_1 \\oplus \\mathcal{H}_2 \\oplus \\mathcal{H}_3 \\oplus \\mathcal{H}_4.\n\\]\n\nWe have \\(\\dim(\\mathcal{H}_N) = \\binom{4}{N}\\) and \\(\\dim(\\mathcal{F}) = 2^4\\).\n\n43--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAspects of Geometric Mechanics and (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/doc